\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Where this survey sits among published radio technosignature searches. \textbf  {(a)} Minimum detectable EIRP against distance: blue, this survey, the deepest window per star; orange, \citet  {Enriquez2017}; aqua, \citet  {Margot2023}; violet, \citet  {Mason2025}; left-edge ticks, \citet  {Price2020}. Grey dotted, EIRP $\propto d^{2}$ at constant received flux; green dashed, the 12\,m benchmark transmitter of \S  \ref  {sec:discussion}. The ordinates are commensurable as total powers for a sub-channel carrier; what differs between programmes is the detection threshold and the completeness level each adopts, and this survey's value is the stricter of the two conventions. \textbf  {(b)} Native channel width against observing frequency for every searched window (filled, Class\nobreakspace  {}A; open, Class\nobreakspace  {}B), with the comparison programmes as horizontal segments. The channel widths span about seven orders of magnitude, which is what the $\Delta \qopname  \relax o{log}\Delta \nu _{\rm  ch}\approx 7$ annotation marks: the channelisation, not the plotted power, is what differs most between programmes.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:context}{{1}{3}{Where this survey sits among published radio technosignature searches. \textbf {(a)} Minimum detectable EIRP against distance: blue, this survey, the deepest window per star; orange, \citet {Enriquez2017}; aqua, \citet {Margot2023}; violet, \citet {Mason2025}; left-edge ticks, \citet {Price2020}. Grey dotted, EIRP $\propto d^{2}$ at constant received flux; green dashed, the \BenchDiam \,m benchmark transmitter of \S \ref {sec:discussion}. The ordinates are commensurable as total powers for a sub-channel carrier; what differs between programmes is the detection threshold and the completeness level each adopts, and this survey's value is the stricter of the two conventions. \textbf {(b)} Native channel width against observing frequency for every searched window (filled, Class~A; open, Class~B), with the comparison programmes as horizontal segments. The channel widths span about seven orders of magnitude, which is what the $\Delta \log \Delta \nu _{\rm ch}\approx 7$ annotation marks: the channelisation, not the plotted power, is what differs most between programmes}{figure.1}{}}
